Dear Students,
A few important reminders about our NLP course:

Tutorials are mandatory. Attendance is required, including scheduled
project meetings with your supervisors, as metioned in the course
rules.
Re-read the course rules, particularly the assessment criteria,
submission requirements, deadlines, and grading procedures.
Phase 1 rebuttal: If you disagree with your grade, provide
substantial, evidence-based arguments for each assessment criterion
you challenge. Refer to specific sections and page numbers in your
submitted report.
Claims require evidence. Simply disagreeing with the feedback is not
sufficient. Explain precisely why the assigned score does not reflect
your submitted work.
Document important decisions. Record agreements made during meetings
and share them with the relevant people (e.g., the main course
coordinator). Undocumented decisions are difficult to verify.

Rebuttal is part of your Phase 2 assessment
According to the published grading criteria, Criterion 2.4 — Rebuttal
to the supervisors' reviews and Q&A questions — is worth 3 points in
Phase 2 (20 points total).
In your updated Phase 2 report, you are expected to:

Address every comment from your supervisors' reviews and every
question your team received during the mid-term Q&A.
Clearly indicate whether each comment was accepted and addressed,
rejected with justification, or deferred with an explanation.
Provide substantial arguments and evidence supporting your decisions.
Revise your report accordingly, indicating the exact sections, tables,
figures, or experiments that have been changed.
If you disagree with a recommendation, explain your reasoning using
relevant research, methodological arguments, or empirical evidence.

Simply acknowledging feedback is not sufficient. Your rebuttal must
demonstrate critical reflection and lead to justified improvements in
your work.
Phase 2 deadline: December 2, 2026.
Why does this matter?
Scientific reasoning, research integrity, reproducibility,
professional communication, and critical collaboration with AI are
important learning outcomes of this course. These are competencies
expected at the Master's degree level and should be developed and
assessed in graduate-level Data Science courses.
Not everyone will pursue an academic career. However, the ability to
justify methodological choices, critically evaluate results, document
decisions, respond professionally to feedback, and support conclusions
with evidence is equally essential in industry. In professional Data
Science, you may need to defend your model selection, justify
evaluation metrics, challenge incorrect analyses, or explain
limitations to colleagues, clients, and stakeholders.
Fairness and AI-assisted assessment
Our assessment process combines AI-assisted analysis with human
academic judgment. Both AI systems and human reviewers can make
mistakes.
I am happy to reconsider scores when convincing evidence demonstrates
an assessment error. However, I must also ensure that the same rules
are applied consistently and fairly across all teams. Any proposed
correction therefore requires verification.
Please also distinguish between the scored assessment criteria and
additional formative feedback. Not every recommendation results in a
deduction.

I hope you will approach this phase not simply as an opportunity to
negotiate grades, but as an exercise in scientific reasoning, research
integrity, professional Data Science practice, and critical
collaboration with AI.
Your ability to critically assess feedback, defend your decisions, and
improve your work is itself an important part of what you are expected
to learn.
I welcome well-supported disagreements and constructive discussions.
Best regards,

Anna Wróblewska
