<body><p>Dear Students,</p>
<p>A few important reminders about our NLP course:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Tutorials are mandatory.</strong> Attendance is required, including scheduled project meetings with your supervisors, as metioned in the course rules.</li>
<li><strong>Re-read the course rules</strong>, particularly the assessment criteria, submission requirements, deadlines, and grading procedures.</li>
<li><strong>Phase 1 rebuttal:</strong> If you disagree with your grade, provide substantial, evidence-based arguments <strong>for each assessment criterion you challenge</strong>. Refer to specific sections and page numbers in your submitted report.</li>
<li><strong>Claims require evidence.</strong> Simply disagreeing with the feedback is not sufficient. Explain precisely why the assigned score does not reflect your submitted work.</li>
<li><strong>Document important decisions.</strong> Record agreements made during meetings and share them with the relevant people (e.g., the main course coordinator). Undocumented decisions are difficult to verify.</li>
</ol>
<h3 id="rebuttalispartofyourphase2assessment">Rebuttal is part of your Phase 2 assessment</h3>
<p>According to the published grading criteria, <strong>Criterion 2.4 — Rebuttal to the supervisors' reviews and Q&amp;A questions — is worth 3 points in Phase 2 (20 points total).</strong></p>
<p>In your updated Phase 2 report, you are expected to:</p>
<ul>
<li>Address <strong>every comment</strong> from your supervisors' reviews and every question your team received during the mid-term Q&amp;A.</li>
<li>Clearly indicate whether each comment was <strong>accepted and addressed, rejected with justification, or deferred with an explanation</strong>.</li>
<li>Provide substantial arguments and evidence supporting your decisions.</li>
<li><strong>Revise your report accordingly</strong>, indicating the exact sections, tables, figures, or experiments that have been changed.</li>
<li>If you disagree with a recommendation, explain your reasoning using relevant research, methodological arguments, or empirical evidence.</li>
</ul>
<p>Simply acknowledging feedback is not sufficient. Your rebuttal must demonstrate critical reflection and lead to justified improvements in your work.</p>
<p><strong>Phase 2 deadline: December 2, 2026.</strong></p>
<h3 id="whydoesthismatter">Why does this matter?</h3>
<p>Scientific reasoning, research integrity, reproducibility, professional communication, and critical collaboration with AI are important <strong>learning outcomes of this course</strong>. These are competencies expected at the <strong>Master's degree level</strong> and should be developed and assessed in graduate-level Data Science courses.</p>
<p>Not everyone will pursue an academic career. However, the ability to justify methodological choices, critically evaluate results, document decisions, respond professionally to feedback, and support conclusions with evidence is equally essential in industry. In professional Data Science, you may need to defend your model selection, justify evaluation metrics, challenge incorrect analyses, or explain limitations to colleagues, clients, and stakeholders.</p>
<h3 id="fairnessandaiassistedassessment">Fairness and AI-assisted assessment</h3>
<p>Our assessment process combines AI-assisted analysis with human academic judgment. Both AI systems and human reviewers can make mistakes.</p>
<p><strong>I am happy to reconsider scores when convincing evidence demonstrates an assessment error. However, I must also ensure that the same rules are applied consistently and fairly across all teams. Any proposed correction therefore requires verification.</strong></p>
<p>Please also distinguish between the scored assessment criteria and additional formative feedback. Not every recommendation results in a deduction. <br>
I hope you will approach this phase not simply as an opportunity to negotiate grades, but as an exercise in <strong>scientific reasoning, research integrity, professional Data Science practice, and critical collaboration with AI</strong>.</p>
<p>Your ability to critically assess feedback, defend your decisions, and improve your work is itself an important part of what you are expected to learn.</p>
<p>I welcome well-supported disagreements and constructive discussions.</p>
<p>Best regards,<br>
Anna Wróblewska</p><p>-- <br>Wiadomość wysłana przez system USOS.<br>Łączna liczba adresatów tej wiadomości: 77</p></body>
